# 3Sum — LeetCode 15

**Pattern:** Sorting + Two Pointers  
**Approach:** Fix one element and solve Two Sum for the remaining array.

## Approach
1. Sort the array.
2. Iterate through the array, fixing `nums[i]`.
3. Skip duplicate fixed elements: `if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue`.
4. Set `target = -nums[i]`, `low = i + 1`, `high = n - 1`.
5. If `nums[low] + nums[high] == target`:
   - Add the triplet to `ans`.
   - Move both pointers inward.
   - Skip duplicate values for both pointers.
6. If the sum is greater than target, decrement `high`.
7. Otherwise, increment `low`.

## Java Code

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        int n = nums.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1])
                continue;

            int target = -nums[i];
            twoSum(i, nums, target, n, ans);
        }

        return ans;
    }

    public void twoSum(int i, int[] nums, int target,
                       int n, List<List<Integer>> ans) {
        int low = i + 1;
        int high = n - 1;

        while (low < high) {
            int sum = nums[low] + nums[high];

            if (sum == target) {
                ans.add(Arrays.asList(
                    nums[i], nums[low], nums[high]
                ));

                low++;
                high--;

                while (low < high &&
                       nums[low] == nums[low - 1])
                    low++;

                while (low < high &&
                       nums[high] == nums[high + 1])
                    high--;

            } else if (sum > target) {
                high--;
            } else {
                low++;
            }
        }
    }
}
